Google's A2A (Agent-to-Agent) Protocol and Anthropic's MCP (Model Context Protocol) are both designed to enhance interoperability in the agentic AI ecosystem. While MCP focuses on enabling communication between AI models or agents, particularly through exposing portable tools that can be used by these entities, A2A aims at facilitating seamless collaboration among diverse agentic applications.

The innovative aspect of A2A lies in its provision of a common language for agent interoperability, regardless of underlying frameworks or vendors' implementations. This protocol enables developers to build their own agents using any framework but ensures they can communicate effectively with other agents via A2A. By doing so, it addresses fragmentation within the technological landscape and enhances autonomy and capabilities of individual AI agents through collaborative interactions.

Specific problems addressed by A2A include reducing fragmentation within the agentic AI ecosystem and supporting broader application scenarios where multiple specialized AI agents need to work together seamlessly. It allows remote and local agents as well as humans to interact securely, thereby fostering more cohesive workflows across different domains and businesses.

In summary, while MCP emphasizes detailed model context sharing and interaction, A2A takes a more holistic approach towards general inter-agency communications. Both protocols contribute significantly to advancing the field of agentic AI by promoting modularity, portability, and seamless integration among various autonomous systems.
